{"type":"message_update","usage":{"input":3198,"output":5577,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":0,"totalTokens":8775,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"Okay, I've read both files. Now let's examine the requirements in `src/ledger.py`'s docstring and the tests.\n\nDocstring requirements for `summarize_ledger(csv_text)`:\n1.  Total the amount column per category.\n2.  Returns `{category: total}`.\n3.  Each total rounded to 2 decimals, using **round-half-up** (accounting convention), not Python's default round-half-to-even.\n4.  Columns: `date,category,description,amount`.\n5.  Category keys: lowercase with surrounding whitespace stripped.\n6.  Description field: may contain commas and RFC4180-escaped internal quotes (doubled `\"\"` represents one literal `\"`).\n7.  Amounts:\n    -   May be quoted.\n    -   May use `$`, `€`, or `£` symbols.\n    -   May use thousands separators (e.g., `,`).\n    -   May have a third decimal place (e.g., `0.125`).\n    -   Negative amount: either in accounting parentheses `(45.00)` or with a leading minus sign `-45.00`.\n8.  Rows to skip:\n    -   Rows with a missing or empty amount.\n    -   Blank lines.\n    -   Rows that do not have exactly four fields once quoting is accounted for (e.g. footer lines).\n\nThe current implementation of `summarize_ledger` is very naive:\n```python\ndef summarize_ledger(csv_text):\n    # ... (docstring)\n    totals = {}\n    for line in csvint.strip().splitlines()[1:]:\n        parts = line.split(\",\")\n        cat = parts[1]\n        amount = float(parts[3].replace(\"$\", \"\"))\n        totals[cat] = totals.get(cat, 0) + amount\n    return {k: round(v, 2) for k, v in totals.items()}\n```\n\nIt fails on:\n-   Quotes in the CSV (it doesn't use a proper CSV parser).\n-   `€`, `£` symbols.\n-   Thousands separators.\n-   Accounting parentheses for negative numbers.\n-   Round-half-up (it uses `round()`, which is round-half-to-even in Python).\n-   Whitespace stripping for categories.\n-   Handling of other currencies.\n-   Skipping invalid rows (footer lines).\n-   Case sensitivity (needs lowercase).\n\nI should use the `csv` module to parse the CSV correctly, as it handles quotes and commas within quotes.\n\nFor round-half-up:\nThe standard way to do round-half-up in Python is using `Decimal` with `ROUND_HALF_UP`.\n\nLet's plan the implementation.\n\n1.  Use `io.StringIO` and `csv.reader`.\n2.  Iterate through the rows.\n3.  Skip the header.\n4.  For each row:\n    -   Check if it has exactly 4 columns. If not, skip.\n    -   Extract category: `row[1].strip().lower()`.\n    lar\n    -   Extract amount string: `row[3]`.\n    -   Clean the amount string:\n        -   Remove whitespace.\n        -   Remove currency symbols (`$`, `€`, `£`).\n        -   Remove thousands separators (`,`).\n        -   Handle accounting parentheses: if it starts with `(` and ends with `)`, replace with `-` and remove parentheses.\n    -   Parse the cleaned amount string into a `Decimal`.\n    -   If amount string is empty or parsing fails, skip.\n    -   Add to `totals` dict.\n5.  After processing all rows, round each total to 2 decimal places using `ROUND_HALF_UP`.\n6.  Convert `Decimal` back to `float` if necessary, or just return `float` as the test expects (the test compares with `0.005` tolerance, and the values in the test are floats). The test uses `abs(r.get(...) - ...) < 0.005`, so `float` should be fine.\n\nWait, let's re-read the amount cleaning requirements.\n\"Amounts may be quoted, may use $, EUR (€), or GBP (£) symbols, thousands separators, and may have a third decimal place (a sub-cent remainder from currency conversion). A negative amount is written either in accounting parentheses, e.g. (45.00), or with a leading minus sign.\"\n\nLet's refine the amount parsing logic:\n```python\nfrom decimal import Decimal, ROUND_HALF_UP\nimport csv\nimport io\n\ndef s